# Python quality

Match tooling in `pyproject.toml` — do not fight ruff or mypy.

## Types

- Imports at module top; no inline imports unless circular dependency is documented
- Annotate all public functions, methods, and module-level names in `src/happy_housing`
- Prefer `pathlib.Path`, explicit returns, `X | Y` over `Optional`
- **No** `from __future__ import annotations` unless required (unquoted forward
  refs that fail at definition time on Python 3.12). Prefer quoted
  ``"ForwardName"`` / reorder definitions instead of a blanket future import.
  Also quote annotations that use ``TYPE_CHECKING``-only imports or
  non-subscriptable runtime types (e.g. ``"gpd.GeoDataFrame"``, ``"tqdm[Any]"``)
- No bare `except:`; no `# type: ignore` without a comment
- Ruff line length 88 — run the formatter
- Exhaustive `match` on unions: `never` in the default branch
- No bare `*` for keyword-only parameters unless required — e.g. multiple optional args where positional calls would be ambiguous, or a deliberate API boundary. Do not add `*` just to mark one trailing parameter as keyword-only.

## Docstrings (Google style)

Use [Google-style](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#383-functions-and-methods) docstrings in `src/happy_housing`. Types live in annotations — **do not repeat them** in `Args`/`Returns`.

Every Python file in `src/happy_housing` starts with a module docstring — including `__init__.py`. Every module-level constant gets a docstring on the line immediately below its definition.

| Surface | Rule |
|---------|------|
| Module | **Required** on every file (including `__init__.py`) |
| Module-level constant | **Required**: one-line docstring directly under the assignment |
| Public class | One-line summary; expand only when behavior is non-obvious |
| Public function/method | One line when signature is sufficient; else `Args`/`Returns`/`Raises` |
| `_private` | Omit unless logic is genuinely non-obvious |
| `tests/` | Docstring optional; prefer clear test names |

Comments: non-obvious logic only.

## Logging

Use the stdlib [`logging`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html) package — no `print` for diagnostics in library code.

- One module logger per file: `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`
- No handlers in library modules unless the experiment explicitly configures logging at the entrypoint
